"turboblake 1.8.7 - Docs.rs (https://docs.rs/crate/turboblake/1.8.7)\nciteturn7search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   *     * 1.8.7 (2026-10-26) ...   * turboblake-1.8.7 is not a library.\n\n  * Versions\n  *     * 1.8.7 (2026-10-26)\n\n  * Owners\n  * turboblake-1.8.7 is not a library.\n\n# turboblake\n\nBLAKE3, turbocharged: the official `b3sum` (1.8.7) on `blake3-bend`, a drop-in for `b3sum` and `sha256sum`. Every flag, every output format and every error message is upstream's (upstream's own CLI tests pass), and in the default mode many files and big ones are hashed at once by a Bend2 engine: opened in parallel, mapped or read, files not in the page cache streamed at SSD speed, every file's tree on every core. BLAKE3 is a Merkle tree, and Bend2 runs tree-shaped work in parallel on every CPU core and the GPU.\n    \n    `brew install gunta/tap/turboblake\n    `\n    \n    `cargo install turboblake\n    `\n\nHomebrew installs a prebuilt binary (macOS and Linux, arm64 and x86-64); `cargo install` builds from source, tuned for your CPU, and needs clang (Apple's, or LLVM 19+). `cargo binstall turboblake` fetches the prebuilt binaries through cargo.\n    \n    `turboblake FILE...           # one \"<hash>  <name>\" line per file, as b3sum prints\n    turboblake FILE... > B3SUMS  # write checksums\n    turboblake --check B3SUMS    # verify them, like sha256sum -c\n    `\n\nTo make it your `b3sum` (it takes the place of the official one, if you had installed that with cargo):\n    \n    `cargo install turboblake --features replace --force\n    `\n\nor keep both and alias it: `alias b3sum=turboblake`.\n\n`--keyed`, `--derive-key`, `--length`, `--seek`, `--check`, `--no-mmap` and stdin go through upstream's code (on blake3-bend, so a big input still takes every core); `BLAKE3_BEND=0` turns the engine off. Error messages start with `turboblake:`, or `b3sum:` for the `replace` install.\n\nAgainst the official `b3sum` on an Apple M5 Pro: a 101.5 GB DeepSeek-V4.1 model shard bigger than RAM in 7.65 s against 211 s; 2,000 x 64 KiB files 10.7x faster, 8,000 cargo source files 3.5x. Benchmarks and method: gunta.github.io/BLAKE3-bend.\n\nLicense: CC0-1.0 OR Apache-2.0 OR Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception, as BLAKE3. The engine contains the Bend2 runtime, Apache-2.0.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCryptology ePrint Archive [Mirror] (https://eprint.iacr.asia/2026?offset=100)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; 2026/2468 (PDF) ... FlatMerkle: Logarithmic-Space Inclusion Proofs over a Single Linear Hash Stream\n2026/2468 (PDF)\n\nLast updated:  2026-10-14\n\nFlatMerkle: Logarithmic-Space Inclusion Proofs over a Single Linear Hash Stream\n\nSergio Demian Lerner\n\nShow abstract\n\nCryptographic protocols\n\nMerkle trees are the standard way to obtain logarithmic-size, logarithmic-time inclusion proofs over a large dataset, but they require a tree-shaped hash structure distinct from the ordinary, linear (Merkle--Damg\\aa rd) hashing that most deployed systems -- including Bitcoin's own transaction and witness hashing -- actually perform. We present FlatMerkle, a construction that achieves the same asymptotic guarantees, $O(\\log N)$ proof size and $O(\\log N)$ verification time, over a single ordinary linear hash stream, by periodically re-injecting the hash function's own past internal states (``checkpoints'') back into the stream as ordinary message content. The result is a flat byte sequence whose digest is a single, unmodified invocation of the underlying hash function -- syntactically an ordinary digest of an ordinary byte string, not a distinguishable commitment format -- while still supporting compact inclusion proofs for any element, and compact proofs that a published file is malformed, so that an honest party can always answer a malicious committer in logarithmic space. We give the recursive construction, a closed-form formula locating any element in the structured stream, worked examples with illustrations, a security reduction to free-start collision resistance of the hash function's compression step, an optimal element-size analysis for both a flexible toy primitive and real SHA-256, a batched multi-element proof construction, a measured construction-speed comparison against Merkle trees, a concrete application to committing data inside a Bitcoin transaction annex (BIP-341), a design sketch using that application as a data-availability layer for an optimistic Bitcoin rollup, a from-scratch gas-cost comparison, on Ethereum, Rootstock, and Arbitrum, of an in-contract FlatMerkle commitment against an in-contract Merkle tree, tracing the three chains' differing verdicts to specific protocol design choices rather than to anything intrinsic to either construction, and a comparison against the closest prior art we are aware of, authenticated append-only skip lists.\nSignature length and verification cost are fixed by the parameters and do not depend on the tree height. For CUTS, we compute the two-use coverage exactly as one coefficient of a polynomial and derive deterministic lower bounds on $\\lambda_2$; one parameter set with 1 424-byte signatures has $\\lambda_2\\ge128.6$. A counter variant, CUTS+C, trades a short counter and extra signing work for a proven grinding gain of $g$ bits in the coverage term.\n\n2026/2454 (PDF)\n\nLast updated:  2026-10-12\n\nLocal Leakage Resilience of Shamir Secret Sharing: Below Half Threshold Rate\n\nHai H. Nguyen\n\nShow abstract\n\nFoundations\n\nWe study the local leakage resilience of Shamir secret sharing, where an adversary leaks a bounded number of bits from every share. For $n$ parties and reconstruction threshold $k$ over a prime field $\\mathbb{F}_p$ with $p>n$, we prove the following. (1) There is an absolute constant $c_{\\mathrm{bh}}<1/2$ such that, for $k\\ge c_{\\mathrm{bh}}n$, Shamir sharing is resilient to arbitrary one-bit leakage from every share. The first tool gives the result above half, the second gives the constant-rate result, and applying both to different ranges of supports in the same decomposition gives the result below half.\n\n2026/2453 (PDF)\n\nLast updated:  2026-10-12\n\nRevisiting Witness-Succinct UC-lifting Compilers\n\nDavide Li Calsi, Luigi Russo, Dominique Schröder, and Federico Valbusa\n\nShow abstract\n\nFoundations\n\nZero-knowledge succinct non-interactive arguments of knowledge (zkSNARKs) are increasingly used within larger cryptographic protocols, making security under composition essential. Generic compilation to Universal Composability (UC) requires straight-line, black-box extraction while preserving succinctness and controlling prover cost. We introduce verifiable reconstructible straightline codes (VRSCs), a primitive that separates public hiding, transcript-based reconstruction, binding, and proof non-rerandomizability. This abstraction exposes a missing security requirement in the witness-succinct UC compiler of Ganesh et al. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://eprint-classic.github.io/2026.html)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; # Cryptology ePrint Archive: Listing for 2026 ...   * 2026/2468 (PDF)     FlatMerkle: Logarithmic-Space Inclusion Proofs over a Single Linear Hash Stream\n  * 2026/2473 (PDF)     edaBits in the Exponent (edaBitE) and Issuer-Hiding Blind Signatures from ML-DSA and VOLE-in-the-Head\n    Carsten Baum, Marvin Beckmann\n  * 2026/2472 (PDF)     Nowhere Man: Device Binding for Post-Quantum Anonymous Credentials\n    Calvin Abou Haidar, Ryo Kikuchi, Octavio Perez-Kempner, Marcel Tiepelt\n  * 2026/2471 (PDF)     Connecting Cryptanalytic Distinguishers through Divisibility\n    Hosein Hadipour\n  * 2026/2470 (PDF)     Refined Application of the XL Algorithm within the Algebraic Approach to the Regular Syndrome Decoding Problem\n    Hiroki Furue, Yusuke Aikawa\n  * 2026/2469 (PDF)     Accelerating Pollard Rho with GPU